    About 100++ packages

    A. In this repo you can see, my commits are usually just simple descriptions 
    like rebuild, update, new package addition, optimization, or hotfix. Why?
    Because I package and debug everything alone without any feedback. There’s a 
    lot that needs adjusting, or sometimes I just forget to input a small piece of
    code. If I force an upgrade to a new release in the .spec, there would be way
    too many versions coming out, and they’d only be small improvements based on
    my debugging experience. That’s very inefficient, so I prefer to do rebuilds.

    B. There are some STANDALONE packages. What are those? They’re packages
    patched to be compatible with the DE/WM being used — especially for crucial
    parts like drivers.

    C. About kernel-6.12-rc1.EOL, there’s actually nothing special about it. 
    "Short story" before I got to this many packages, I was confused why so many
    things didn’t work properly compared to popular Linux distros. It turned out
    that a lot of features simply weren’t enabled in kernel-6.6.x. So
    kernel-6.12-rc1.EOL was the first kernel I studied and added to the 
    repository. Note that kernel-6.12-rc1.EOL is a custom kernel I built to test 
    the "zstd" feature. That’s why if you want to install it, you’ll need
    "kmod-30-1STANDALONE" for it to run.

    D. There are a few custom packages that I made myself, including indoterm,
    hyprbar, xfce4-polkit-plugins, and zram.

    E. The packages I provide are quite limited because I tend to repeat or refine
    what already exists instead of wrapping too many packages with weak
    improvements. If the latest commit status says rebuild, just know that I’m 
    perfecting it so that it works flawlessly on a fresh install — but not
    necessarily with upgrades, huffft.

    F. If you need a fast way to get Xfce or Hyprland preconfigured, you can 
    install azurelinux-desktop-xfce-packages or 
    azurelinux-window-hyprland-packages. These are meta-packages that can turn it
    into a ready-to-use Linux system.